   "format": "short_answer",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The function h is defined by h(x) = 4 - 3x for x < -2, h(-2) = 7 + 4k - k^2, and h(x) = k^2 + x + 3 for x > -2, where k is a constant. Find the value of k that makes h continuous at x = -2."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The left-hand limit at x = -2 is 4 - 3(-2) = 10 and the right-hand limit is k^2 - 2 + 3 = k^2 + 1.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            10,
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Power",
                  "k",
                  2
               ],
               1
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Equal one-sided limits need k^2 = 9, so k = 3 or k = -3.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Power",
               "k",
               2
            ],
            9
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The common limit 10 must also equal h(-2) = 7 + 4k - k^2. At k = -3, h(-2) = -14, while at k = 3, h(-2) = 10.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Equal",
            [
               "Add",
               7,
               [
                  "Multiply",
                  4,
                  "k"
               ],
               [
                  "Negate",
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "k",
                     2
                  ]
               ]
            ],
            10
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Only k = 3 makes h continuous at x = -2.",
         "mathjson": 3
      }
   ],
